---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/use-cases/back-in-stock";
const title = "Shopify back-in-stock alerts without false inventory promises";
const desc = "Design variant-specific waitlists, inventory gates and alert waves that tell customers what returned without promising a reservation.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Should restock alerts go to the whole list?",
    "answer": "Serve the relevant requests first under the stated policy. A broader promotional announcement is a separate campaign with its own audience and purpose."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does a waitlist signup reserve a unit?",
    "answer": "Only if the store actually implements and discloses a reservation. A notification or position in a queue alone does not guarantee stock."
  },
  {
    "question": "What if stock sells out after sending?",
    "answer": "Pause unsent alerts, show current availability at the destination and preserve requests according to policy. Distinguish a later sellout from incorrect stock at dispatch."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should old requests expire after a fixed number of days?",
    "answer": "Use a disclosed policy suited to the product lifecycle and applicable requirements. Review discontinued items and offer request cancellation rather than applying an unexplained universal cutoff."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16"><a class="link" href="/use-cases">All use cases</a><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1><p class="mt-6 text-xl">{desc}</p></section>
  <section class="container pb-16"><article class="card prose-shopify">

<h2>Capture a request for the right item</h2>
<p>A restock signup is an expression of interest in a particular unavailable item. Store the product and variant identifiers, contact destination, signup time, requested channel and permission record. Show the chosen size, color or edition back to the shopper. Deduplicate repeated requests for the same variant without silently converting a restock request into a general newsletter subscription.</p>
<p>State what the signup means: notification of availability, queue priority or a genuine reservation. These are different promises. If the store cannot reserve stock, say so. A limited pressing and a permanently replenished staple may need different alert policies; neither requires an arbitrary link-expiry window.</p>
<h2>Define sellable stock with the warehouse</h2>
<p>Shopify's <a href="https://shopify.dev/docs/api/admin-graphql/latest/objects/InventoryLevel">InventoryLevel reference</a> distinguishes quantity states and locations. Physical on-hand stock and available stock are different concepts. Agree which locations can fulfill the customer market and which quantity state the integration reads. A product-page change alone is not a warehouse confirmation.</p>
<p>Keep damaged units, committed orders and inbound receipts out of an unsupported availability promise. Record inventory freshness and the response to a missing or failed lookup. Ask the connector supplier how it maps item, variant and location data; the existence of Shopify's API does not establish that an email app uses it correctly.</p>
<h2>Choose a fair alert policy</h2>
<p>Decide whether requests are served by signup order, another disclosed priority or a general availability announcement. If inventory is scarce, release a bounded wave and observe remaining availability before releasing the next. Do not imply that signup order guarantees a unit when multiple recipients can compete for the same stock.</p>
<p>Consider a hypothetical shoe restock: one size returns while another remains unavailable. Notify only the matching requests and link to the selected variant. A hypothetical record repress needs an edition identifier; a new pressing is not necessarily the item requested. Offer an explicit alternative when appropriate rather than silently moving requests to a successor product.</p>
<h2>Handle the race between queue and checkout</h2>
<p>Recheck availability before dispatch when the integration supports it. Log the restock event and recipient notification so retries do not create duplicate alerts. A delayed webhook or repeat inventory update should not restart the entire queue. If stock falls to zero, pause unsent alerts and retain eligible requests for the next genuine restock under the published policy.</p>
<p>Availability can change after sending even with a correct send-time check. Link to a page that reflects current stock and clearly says when the item has sold out again. Do not label every post-send sellout a false alert: distinguish incorrect stock at dispatch, sync latency and genuine later demand. Notification is not a stock lock.</p>
<h2>Resolve purchase and journey overlap</h2>
<p>A completed purchase of the requested item should close or update that request according to the stated policy. Buying a substitute does not automatically mean interest in the original disappeared; offer cancellation rather than inventing intent. A cart with the now-available variant can enter <a href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">checkout recovery</a> under its own eligibility rules, with duplicate reminders suppressed.</p>
<p>Keep launch waitlists separate from replenishment. A <a href="/use-cases/product-launch">new-product launch</a> may have advance access, preorder terms and a scheduled release; a restock should communicate actual returned availability. Marketing follow-up after an alert still needs its own purpose and eligibility. Do not automatically enroll every nonbuyer in browse reminders.</p>
<h2>Maintain requests over the product lifecycle</h2>
<p>Give customers a way to cancel a request and explain whether it covers one alert or future restocks. Set retention and revalidation rules based on the product's lifecycle and applicable requirements rather than a universal age cutoff. Review discontinued variants and broken product destinations; do not leave an impossible request active indefinitely.</p>
<p>For seasonal harvests, confirm whether next season is an acceptable alternative before changing the request. For multiple requested variants, consider a combined message only if it preserves each requested identity and destination. Avoid turning a precise stock alert into a generic catalog blast.</p>
<h2>Proposed checks before enabling alerts</h2>
<p>These are acceptance scenarios, not tests performed here: request two variants, restock only one, retry the same event, remove availability while messages are queued, purchase before dispatch, cancel a request and discontinue an item. Add a location that cannot fulfill the intended market. Inspect eligibility, send logs, selected-variant links and queue state after each event.</p>
<p>Ask candidates in the <a href="/apps">app catalog</a>, including Sequenzy if considered, to show capture, inventory ingestion and dispatch as separate stages. Identify the system that owns queue ordering and cancellation. Request the intended plan's integration documentation, latency behavior and outage recovery. A restock template alone does not demonstrate reliable orchestration.</p>
<h2>Measure service quality as well as sales</h2>
<p>Retain requests by variant, alert waves, eligibility and exclusion reasons, available stock snapshots, sends, linked orders and cancellations. Review incorrect-at-dispatch alerts, duplicate notifications, broken destinations and support complaints. Use consistent observation windows for conversion and distinguish first-time from repeat purchasers.</p>
<p>Scarce stock may sell out without any alert, so sell-through alone cannot establish incremental revenue. Treat order credit as descriptive unless a suitable comparison supports more. The <a href="/use-cases/analytics">analytics workflow</a> helps turn the ledger into an operating review. Use the results to improve wave size, request clarity and data reliability, not to claim a universal waitlist conversion target.</p>

  </article></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
